---
import Chapter from "../components/Chapter.astro";
import BackToContents from "../components/BackToContents.astro";
import RustSchema from "../components/RustSchema.astro";
---

<Chapter id="identity-model">
<h2>Identity and presence</h2>
  <p>The protocol uses several identifier namespaces for the same person. Account
  and character identities are stable. Presence IDs and member handles belong to
  one native connection or joined channel.</p>

  <h3 id="identity-chain">Identity path</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Front logon returns a Battle.net <b>account entity ID</b> and one or more
    <b>game-account entity IDs</b>. These are 128-bit protobuf entity values.</li>
    <li>The selected SC2 game account is passed to Game Utilities. Its response
    supplies the native game-account name, region, session key, and native logon
    response used by Resume.</li>
    <li>After Resume, <code>Toon/0</code> lists the playable SC2 identities owned by
    that game account. <code>Toon/5</code> selects one; <code>Toon/6</code> returns
    its stable <code>ToonHandle</code> and profile record address.</li>
    <li><code>Pres/0</code> associates that stable identity and native account data
    with connection-local presence IDs.</li>
    <li><code>Chat/1</code> associates a channel-local member handle with a presence
    ID. The chain can then resolve a message sender to account, toon, profile, and
    display state.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3 id="identity-namespaces">Identifier namespaces</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Identifier</th><th>Scope</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Related namespace</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Front account <code>EntityId</code></td><td>Battle.net account</td><td>The platform account returned by Front logon.</td><td>Native 32-bit account ID.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Front game-account <code>EntityId</code></td><td>One game license/account</td><td>The selected SC2 game account passed to Game Utilities.</td><td>Battle.net account entity or toon.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Native game-account name</td><td>Resume session</td><td>The SC2 game-account string returned by Game Utilities.</td><td>A toon display name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Native <code>account_id</code></td><td>Social services</td><td>A 32-bit Battle.net account key used by Presence, Friends, Profile, and whisper targets.</td><td>Either Front protobuf entity ID.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ToonFullName</code></td><td>Name-based SC2 identity</td><td>Region, program, realm, and display name. <code>Prfl/3</code> resolves it to a handle.</td><td>A stable handle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ToonHandle</code></td><td>Stable SC2 character</td><td>Region, program, realm, and 64-bit toon ID.</td><td>Profile address or account ID.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>RecordAddress</code></td><td>Profile storage</td><td>A label and 64-bit record ID used by <code>Prfl/0</code>.</td><td>Toon handle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Local/master presence ID</td><td>One native connection</td><td>Aliases for an online presence record announced by <code>Pres/0</code>.</td><td>Stable account or toon identity.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Channel index</td><td>One connection</td><td>A three-bit slot assigned to one joined channel.</td><td>Public channel name ID.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Member handle</td><td>One joined channel</td><td>A roster member key used by Chat messages and membership changes.</td><td>Presence ID or account ID.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="identity-cardinality">Identity cardinality</h3>
  <p>One Battle.net account can own multiple game accounts. One SC2 game account can
  expose region- and realm-specific toons. One stable account or toon can appear
  through more than one presence alias, and the same presence can participate in
  several channels under a different member handle in each channel.</p>
  <p>Use a native account ID or toon handle as the durable person key. Merge local
  and master presence IDs announced together.
  When two presence records expose the same nonzero native account ID, fold them to
  the same social identity while retaining every connection-local alias needed for
  routing.</p>

  <h3 id="identity-presence-fields">Presence links</h3>
  <p><code>Pres/1</code> first defines field handles and widths. A later
  <code>Pres/0</code> value can then carry the links below:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Relationship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0x00010005</code></td><td>native account ID</td><td>Primary stable social-account link.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0001001b</code></td><td>native account ID</td><td>Secondary social-account link.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>65561</code></td><td><code>ToonHandle</code></td><td>Stable SC2 character link.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>65556</code></td><td><code>RecordAddress</code></td><td>Profile data and avatar lookup link.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>65555</code></td><td><code>ImageTableEntry</code></td><td>Direct avatar link when present.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p><code>Pres/4</code> starts with a stable <code>ToonHandle</code> and asks the
  server to create temporary presence. Its immediate response reports only a
  result. A later <code>Pres/0</code> supplies the connection-local presence ID.
  That ID can then be used by the <code>LocalPresenceId</code> whisper target.</p>

  <h3 id="identity-chat-link">Channel members</h3>
  <RustSchema code={`struct MembershipChangeJoining {
    member_handle: u32, // local to one joined channel
    presence_id: u32,   // local to the native connection
    statuses: Vec<MemberStatus>,
}`} />
  <p>An incoming <code>Chat/11</code> message names only the channel index and
  member handle. Resolve the member handle through the roster, then resolve its
  presence ID through the Presence store. Only after those two local lookups is it
  safe to associate the message with a native account ID, toon handle, profile
  address, display name, avatar, or standing state.</p>

  <h3 id="identity-profile-links">Profiles and friends</h3>
  <p><code>Prfl/1</code> maps a native account ID to a profile record address.
  <code>Prfl/3</code> maps a <code>ToonFullName</code> to a stable
  <code>ToonHandle</code>. <code>Frnd/6</code> carries the inverse social links in
  bulk: native account ID, full toon name, and profile address. These mappings join
  identity namespaces while preserving the scope of each numeric value.</p>
  <BackToContents />
</Chapter>
